<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Corentin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Corentin of Quimper</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Corentin is a Breton saint venerated as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quimper">Quimper</a>, in Cornouaille in western Brittany, probably in the 5th or 6th century. Legend says he lived as a hermit fed by a miraculous fish that renewed itself each day, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gradlon">King Gradlon</a> gave him land for his see. He is the patron of Quimper, whose cathedral bears his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-corentin.jpg" alt="Saint Corentin" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sjwells53 at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Corentin_Banner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Corentin, also Corentinus and in Breton Kaourintin, is counted among the Seven Saints who evangelised Brittany, with Tugdual de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treguier">Tréguier</a>, Paterne de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samson">Samson</a> of Dol, Pol de Léon, Malo and Brieuc. Tradition holds that these monks came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armorica">Armorica</a> and founded its first bishoprics in the 5th and 6th centuries; the pilgrimage of the Tro Breiz, the tour of Brittany, joins their cities. According to Albert le Grand, Corentin was born in Cornouaille and became a hermit at Plomodiern, beside Ménez-Hom, where he lived on herbs and roots from the forest besides the fish of the legend.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gradlon">King Gradlon</a> decided to create the diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quimper">Quimper</a>, the tradition continues, he asked Corentin to be its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> to be consecrated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-tours">Martin of Tours</a>. He travelled with his disciples Guénolé, the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landevennec-abbey">Landévennec Abbey</a>, and Tudy. Gradlon gave him a palace on the site where Quimper Cathedral now stands, and after his death he was buried before its altar.</p><p>In the Breton legend of the city of Ys, Corentin is the saint who warned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gradlon">Gradlon</a> of the sin of his daughter Dahut, also called Ahes. In the common plot of the tales, king and princess escape the flood on horseback and are met by Corentin, or in some versions by Guénolé, who denounces Dahut, so that she falls into the water and becomes a morgen, or siren. The lost king later finds Corentin's hermitage and is given a morsel of the fish, which the tales take as a figure of the gift of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His life is told in a Vita written around 1220–1235, revised after the discovery of The Ancient Life of Saint Corentin, and the song of Aiquin also tells of him. In 1201 King Philippe Auguste dedicated an abbey near Mantes to him, and in Cornwall St. Corentine's Church at Cury bears his name. He is the patron saint of Cornouaille and of seafood, and his feast falls on 12 December. Images show him holding a whole or half fish, or with one at his feet, sometimes beside a fountain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Corentin of Quimper” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom was ruled by local Breton dynasties and organised around monasteries and the see of Quimper, traditionally founded by <b>Saint Corentin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/cornouaille">Cornouaille</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cornouaille. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quimper">Quimper</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48,-4.1" aria-label="Quimper on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Saint Corentin"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4766 6012h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4766 6012h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=48,-4.1">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
